# Question: does a container a committed config enables run its entrypoint
# alone in pid, mount, network, ipc, uts and cgroup namespaces of its own and
# in a control group of its own, with its loopback device up
# (spec-container-1-runtime AC-1, the container-start test)?
#
# A unit test cannot decide it: the answer is what the patched runtime library
# does on a real kernel, run as root, with init cloned out of the ze binary
# straight into its control group, and what the kernel then shows the
# entrypoint.
#
# Method: the test daemon's container store answers one fixture image holding
# ze-containerprobe, whose run mode writes what it sees from inside to /report
# in its root, so the file lands in the container's writable layer. The verify
# mode, run on the host, waits for that file and compares each fact with what
# a container of its own must see: pid 1, its own cgroup at the root, the
# instance name as host name, lo as the only device and up, and each
# namespace link different from the host's. It prints one line per check and
# exits 1 when one fails. Its reset mode runs first and removes the store, so
# a report an earlier run left behind cannot pass this one.
#
# The store lives under /run, not in the test's work directory: in the QEMU
# guest that directory is on the 9p share of the checkout, and an overlay
# cannot take a 9p directory as its upper layer. $PORT keeps two runs apart.
option=needs-linux:caps=sys-admin
option=exclusive:group=container
option=timeout:value=60s
option=env:var=ze.test.container.store:value=/run/ze-container-test-$PORT

stdin=config:terminator=EOF_CONFIG
container {
	instance probe {
		image probe
	}
}
EOF_CONFIG

cmd=foreground:seq=1:exec=ze-containerprobe reset /run/ze-container-test-$PORT:exit=0
cmd=background:seq=2:exec=ze -:stdin=config
cmd=foreground:seq=3:exec=ze-containerprobe verify /run/ze-container-test-$PORT probe:timeout=45s:exit=0
expect=stdout:contains=probe: pid 1 in its own pid namespace
expect=stdout:contains=probe: its own cgroup reads as the root
expect=stdout:contains=probe: host name is the instance name
expect=stdout:contains=probe: the loopback device is the only device
expect=stdout:contains=probe: the loopback device is up
expect=stdout:contains=probe: a pid namespace other than the host's
expect=stdout:contains=probe: a mnt namespace other than the host's
expect=stdout:contains=probe: a net namespace other than the host's
expect=stdout:contains=probe: a ipc namespace other than the host's
expect=stdout:contains=probe: a uts namespace other than the host's
expect=stdout:contains=probe: a cgroup namespace other than the host's
reject=stdout:contains=probe: FAIL
